        ":question/id": "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-77145",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
    ],
    ":knowledge-point/title": "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"
  }
